{:edb/db-before-t 837 :edb/db-after-t 838 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534150 50 #inst "2026-10-06T05:06:00.837Z" 13194139534150 true] [17592186114925 1100 "Solve the equation $2\\csc x+4=0$ for $0 < x <   2\\pi.$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186114925 1100 "Solve the equation $2\\csc x+4=0$ for $0 < x < 2\\pi$." 13194139534150 false] [17592186114925 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain $0 < x <   2\\pi.$ To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\nx_1 = x + 2\\pi = -\\dfrac \\pi 6 + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Since $\\sin{x}<    0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186114925 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\csc x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2\\csc x &= -4 \\\\\n\\csc x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain $0 < x < 2\\pi$. To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi$:\n\n$$\nx_1 = x + 2\\pi = -\\dfrac \\pi 6 + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x}< 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1566/Source/Images/dad47ac6e5b2ff506997d004ecfa60e6.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}\n$$" 13194139534150 false] [17592186114926 1100 "Solve the equation $2\\csc x + 4 = 0$ for $0 < x \\leq   \\dfrac{7\\pi}{2}.$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186114926 1100 "Solve the equation $2\\csc x + 4 = 0$ for $0 < x \\leq \\dfrac{7\\pi}{2}$." 13194139534150 false] [17592186114926 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi.$\n\n$$\nx_1 = x+2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6.\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Since $\\sin{x}<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the given interval, we need to find angles that are coterminal with $x_1$ or $x_2.$ To do this, we add integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until all solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={x}_{1}+2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{23\\pi }{6} \\\\ {x}_{4} & ={x}_{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{19\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{23\\pi}{6}$ is outside the required domain, so we ignore it.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6},\\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{19\\pi}{6}.$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186114926 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\csc x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2\\csc x &= -4 \\\\\n\\csc x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi$.\n\n$$\nx_1 = x+2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x}<0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1566/Source/Images/1ea3d46403714f4a6fd27389cf88708d.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the given interval, we need to find angles that are coterminal with $x_1$ or $x_2$. To do this, we add integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until all solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{3} &= x_{1} + 2π = \\frac{11π}{6} + 2π = \\frac{23π}{6} \\\\\nx_{4} &= x_{2} + 2π = \\frac{7π}{6} + 2π = \\frac{19π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{23\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nis outside the required domain, so we ignore it.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6},\\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{19\\pi}{6}\n$$" 13194139534150 false] [17592186114968 1100 "Find, in radians to $1$ decimal place, all of the solutions to the equation $\\dfrac{\\csc x}{5} -1 = 0$ for $0 \\leq x <              2\\pi.$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186114968 1100 "Find, in radians to $1$ decimal place, all of the solutions to the equation $\\dfrac{\\csc x}{5} -1 = 0$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$." 13194139534150 false] [17592186114968 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{5}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})=0.201\\,357…≈0.201\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.201$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 0.201.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 0.201.$ Since $\\sin{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 0.201.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/8bcd1ea412f9235a330f37ee9059a023d0ce9e03e2c38388875dc0c12afebc1e.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -0.201 \\\\  & ≈2.941\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are $x = 0.2, 2.9.$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186114968 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\csc x}{5} - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\csc x}{5} &= 1 \\\\\n\\csc x &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\ntherefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin (\\frac{1}{5}) = 0.201357… \\approx 0.201\n$$\n\nSince $0.201$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0.201\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.201\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.201\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1566/Source/Images/ed103d88ba3ff90d3c2bd12986afbef5.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π - 0.201 \\\\\n&\\approx 2.941\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are\n\n$$\nx = 0.2, 2.9\n$$" 13194139534150 false] [17592186114969 1100 "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ for $-2\\pi \\leq x <           2\\pi.$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186114969 1100 "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ for $-2\\pi \\leq x < 2\\pi$." 13194139534150 false] [17592186114969 1145 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no solution because the sine function always satisfies\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq 1\n$$\n\nand the value\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nis outside of this range.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nhas no solution." 13194139534150 false] [17592186114969 1145 "Recall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =\\dfrac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no solution because the sine function always satisfies $-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq 1,$ and the value $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is outside of this range.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ has no solution." 13194139534150 true] [17592186115099 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278800 13194139534150 true] [17592186115099 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278841 13194139534150 true] [17592186115099 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278862 13194139534150 true] [17592186115099 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278893 13194139534150 true] [17592186115100 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278835 13194139534150 true] [17592186115100 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278855 13194139534150 true] [17592186115100 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278876 13194139534150 true] [17592186115100 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278881 13194139534150 true] [17592186115100 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278907 13194139534150 true] [17592186115141 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278807 13194139534150 true] [17592186115141 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278814 13194139534150 true] [17592186115141 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278865 13194139534150 true] [17592186115141 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278871 13194139534150 true] [17592186115141 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278900 13194139534150 true] [17592186115142 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278821 13194139534150 true] [17592186115142 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278828 13194139534150 true] [17592186115142 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278848 13194139534150 true] [17592186115142 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278886 13194139534150 true] [17592186115620 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534150 true] [17592186115620 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\csc x = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0 \\le x < 2π$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?" 13194139534150 false] [17592186115620 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186115620 1145 "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/4cbf970747a9eddbcffe6fd3ba46d13c43e5f17ca56e52747c797229dbc74d48.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186115623 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534150 true] [17592186115623 1100 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0 \\le x < 2π$?\n\n1. $\\csc x = 10\\frac{1}{2}$\n2. $\\csc x = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{1}{2}$\n3. $\\csc x =-1\\frac{1}{2}$" 13194139534150 false] [17592186115623 1100 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186115623 1145 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation II has no solutions." 13194139534150 true] [17592186278800 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278801 13194139534150 true] [17592186278800 1089 "q-120532" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278800 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534150 true] [17592186278800 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3=-1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278800 1113 #uuid "398ff4f2-a514-55ba-ad0a-235934ea5e85" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278800 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3 & =-1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value: \n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}.$ \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={330}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={330}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/b3c523e882baf0b3a105e10131091e40de686b4c1483f46e7bdc235e30743a6d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={210}^{∘},{330}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=210\\cdot 330=69\\,300.\n$$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278802 13194139534150 true] [17592186278801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278803 13194139534150 true] [17592186278801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278804 13194139534150 true] [17592186278801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278805 13194139534150 true] [17592186278801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278806 13194139534150 true] [17592186278801 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534150 true] [17592186278801 1041 "selection" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278801 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278803 13194139534150 true] [17592186278801 1110 #uuid "ec5e2113-8d5c-5ce5-973f-2c3eaf6348ca" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278802 1034 "36\\,000" 13194139534150 true] [175921