adrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/4cb8f27f6c108091d233ec0f2a2dbb3439c7df93747917e6a50db770642e76f8.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\nx={60}^{∘},\\,x={120}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278837 13194139534150 true] [17592186278836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278838 13194139534150 true] [17592186278836 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534150 true] [17592186278836 1041 "field-1" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278836 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278837 13194139534150 true] [17592186278836 1110 #uuid "6d9cf82c-d1a6-523c-8154-a37bbaaf4fd9" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278837 1034 "60" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278837 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534150 true] [17592186278837 1111 #uuid "397b6836-1954-5965-a677-7c863d978740" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278838 1034 "-60" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278838 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534150 true] [17592186278838 1111 #uuid "2ade87a4-b691-5e0d-ac43-408b4b408416" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278839 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278840 13194139534150 true] [17592186278839 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534150 true] [17592186278839 1041 "field-2" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278839 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278840 13194139534150 true] [17592186278839 1110 #uuid "d064a5ca-3ff4-5952-80d6-7a2dbbc0af00" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278840 1034 "120" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534150 true] [17592186278840 1111 #uuid "3ff364d7-4c5a-5647-b344-b7b2bb3ae3fa" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278841 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278842 13194139534150 true] [17592186278841 1089 "q-29005" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278841 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534150 true] [17592186278841 1100 "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278841 1113 #uuid "3cae031a-1582-5f4f-9d3f-2d2de0fa261a" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278841 1145 "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is not in the range $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution." 13194139534150 true] [17592186278842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278843 13194139534150 true] [17592186278842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278844 13194139534150 true] [17592186278842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278845 13194139534150 true] [17592186278842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278846 13194139534150 true] [17592186278842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278847 13194139534150 true] [17592186278842 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534150 true] [17592186278842 1041 "selection" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278842 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278844 13194139534150 true] [17592186278842 1110 #uuid "7ba48b2e-8ca6-52b