 follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{48.5904}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{228.5904}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{228.6}^{∘},\\,x≈{311.4}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278872 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278873 13194139534150 true] [17592186278872 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534150 true] [17592186278872 1041 "field-1" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278872 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278873 13194139534150 true] [17592186278872 1110 #uuid "300a53e6-339d-52dd-84d5-dc4bd842d2cd" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278873 1034 "228.6" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278873 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534150 true] [17592186278873 1111 #uuid "73842a9f-f874-58d4-8f18-d5f11b53dcf8" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278874 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278875 13194139534150 true] [17592186278874 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534150 true] [17592186278874 1041 "field-2" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278874 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278875 13194139534150 true] [17592186278874 1110 #uuid "77a9b002-abf2-5298-b074-0e8e8275d974" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278875 1034 "311.4" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278875 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534150 true] [17592186278875 1111 #uuid "f3bda4f3-c595-519d-aea4-102cb88ef461" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278876 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278877 13194139534150 true] [17592186278876 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278879 13194139534150 true] [17592186278876 1089 "q-338798" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278876 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534150 true] [17592186278876 1100 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\sqrt{2}$ for $-2\\pi \\le x<0,$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139534150 true] [17592186278876 1113 #uuid "f5ce8b78-f898-5b03-a908-0659afcbaed8" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278876 1145 "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/012f0986293e2a4318dc68633a4ac0e91732d74300496427aaaa6635b417efe4.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ is not in the required domain $-2\\pi \\le x\\le 0.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order,  the solutions are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278877 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278878 13194139534150 true] [17592186278877 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534150 true] [17592186278877 1041 "field-1" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278877 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278878 13194139534150 true] [17592186278877 1110 #uuid "6725808f-d1c3-525a-8896-7b59c87abd21" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278878 1034 "-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278878 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534150 true] [17592186278878 1111 #uuid "ee01796e-e398-5fdd-9f22-40dc37629b69" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278880 13194139534150 true] [17592186278879 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534150 true] [17592186278879 1041 "field-2" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278879 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278880 13194139534150 true] [17592186278879 1110 #uuid "08e1d500-fbff-5052-9390-7c55d955ba5d" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278880 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi}{4}" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278880 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534150 true] [17592186278880 1111 #uuid "5da2b12d-8aca-54bf-b633-9f6f38393b3d" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278881 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278882 13194139534150 true] [17592186278881 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278884 13194139534150 true] [17592186278881 1089 "q-338802" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278881 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534150 true] [17592186278881 1100 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2\\sqrt{3},$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278881 1113 #uuid "22a6864e-ab1d-5bbc-9659-d70aedb84665" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278881 1145 "First, we rearrange the given equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and 