eratorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies in the required domain, the first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/a45a91dfd90e1fe477d562486d641e4932722ca62645c3eb4024c4f851c964d3.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278909 13194139534150 true] [17592186278908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278910 13194139534150 true] [17592186278908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278911 13194139534150 true] [17592186278908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278912 13194139534150 true] [17592186278908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186278913 13194139534150 true] [17592186278908 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534150 true] [17592186278908 1041 "selection" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278908 1104 #edb/ref 17592186278913 13194139534150 true] [17592186278908 1110 #uuid "a750051f-2bf7-52ee-8dce-11d90f3717eb" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278909 1034 "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278909 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534150 true] [17592186278909 1111 #uuid "c249a4a5-081c-5ac7-a92d-b36b54b3bfb9" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278910 1034 "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" 13194139534150 true] [17592186278910 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534150 true] [17592186278910 1111 